<Project>
  <!--
    Shared settings for the native side of PCMode.

    This directory is the standalone native Windows application: its own
    versioned configuration and state under %LOCALAPPDATA%\PCMode\Native, its own
    engine, its own providers, its own picker and input. It starts no other
    process and reads no other implementation's files.

    TargetFramework is deliberately NOT set here. It genuinely differs (the
    picker and its tests are plain net10.0 so they cannot reference a window
    at all, while the engine and the shell are net10.0-windows), and a shared
    default that half the projects override is a value nobody can trust.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

    <!-- A warning here is a defect that has not happened yet. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <NeutralLanguage>en</NeutralLanguage>

    <!--
      Globalization-invariant mode is OFF, and stating it is not decoration.

      It was on, and it stopped the tray from starting at all. In invariant
      mode the invariant culture is the only one that exists, so
      CultureInfo.GetCultureInfo throws for every named culture. WPF cannot
      live with that. FrameworkElement.Language defaults to the hard-coded tag
      "en-US" (a WPF constant, not anything the user picked), and every binding
      resolves its culture through XmlLanguage.GetSpecificCulture(). The lookup
      of "en-us" threw, the fallback to "en" threw, and the first binding
      evaluated during InitializeComponent came back out as a XamlParseException
      on Selector.SelectedItem wrapping "Cannot find non-neutral culture related
      to 'en-us'", which killed OnStartup. Because the tag is a constant, this
      happened on every machine in every region. It only looked like a locale
      problem.

      It applies to the tests too, deliberately. A test project configured
      differently from the application it is the oracle for cannot be trusted
      to catch this class of defect, and that is precisely how this one
      survived: both were invariant together, so every ShellViewModel test
      passed while the window could not be built at all.

      Nothing here relies on ambient culture for correctness. Where culture
      matters, which is reading and writing the configuration and state
      documents, it is pinned explicitly at the call site. That is the only
      place a guarantee can actually hold, because a build switch in one
      project says nothing about the machine it eventually runs on.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
